WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:08.340
LYNN LANGIT: Hi, I'm Lynn
Langit, cloud architect

00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:12.420
and developer and the first
Google Developer Expert,

00:00:12.420 --> 00:00:14.080
or GDE.

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:18.020
Lately, I've been working
with bioinformatics clients

00:00:18.020 --> 00:00:21.160
building cloud-based
genomic-scale pipelines.

00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:27.510
In this talk, I'm going
to talk about patterns

00:00:27.510 --> 00:00:30.300
for implementing effective
pipelines on the Google Cloud

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:31.250
Platform.

00:00:31.250 --> 00:00:34.850
There are four
parts, what is needed

00:00:34.850 --> 00:00:39.600
in this area, why do we
need to have patterns

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:42.120
and how do you
learn them, how are

00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:44.630
you going to use
pattern information,

00:00:44.630 --> 00:00:47.130
and how can you learn more.

00:00:47.130 --> 00:00:51.510
First we need to understand the
scale and scope of the problem.

00:00:51.510 --> 00:00:53.880
In my work as a
cloud architect, I've

00:00:53.880 --> 00:00:56.220
worked with some
pretty big data sets

00:00:56.220 --> 00:01:00.450
in fintech in New York and
London, in adtech in LA,

00:01:00.450 --> 00:01:05.319
but genomic-scale data
sets are a whole new level.

00:01:05.319 --> 00:01:08.310
The amount of data
that's being generated

00:01:08.310 --> 00:01:13.800
by mostly human genomic
analysis requires new patterns

00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:17.055
for building effective analysis
pipelines in the cloud.

00:01:17.055 --> 00:01:18.930
Now, when I talk about
scale, what do I mean?

00:01:18.930 --> 00:01:20.270
Can I give an example?

00:01:20.270 --> 00:01:22.328
Yeah, I will.

00:01:22.328 --> 00:01:24.120
A recent client that
I've been working with

00:01:24.120 --> 00:01:27.750
is the Broad Institute
at MIT and Harvard.

00:01:27.750 --> 00:01:34.470
On average, they've been
putting in 17 terabytes per day

00:01:34.470 --> 00:01:36.830
into the Google Cloud.

00:01:36.830 --> 00:01:40.210
This is an astounding number.

00:01:40.210 --> 00:01:43.920
Think about it in your own
work in data pipelining.

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:45.780
Are any of you
working at anywhere

00:01:45.780 --> 00:01:47.890
close to the scale yet?

00:01:47.890 --> 00:01:50.658
If not, I think you will be,
even if you're not in genomics.

00:01:50.658 --> 00:01:52.200
And the reason for
this is the amount

00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:54.900
of data that's being generated.

00:01:54.900 --> 00:01:57.900
I've had other projects
in the IoT area in adtech,

00:01:57.900 --> 00:01:59.970
and they come close to this.

00:01:59.970 --> 00:02:03.190
But genomics is at
the cutting edge.

00:02:03.190 --> 00:02:07.290
So what are the challenges
in building effective GCP

00:02:07.290 --> 00:02:09.130
pipelines at this scale?

00:02:09.130 --> 00:02:10.620
Well, there is a lot of them.

00:02:10.620 --> 00:02:14.400
First, cloud practices--
which services

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:16.110
should our researchers use?

00:02:16.110 --> 00:02:17.820
Should they use
virtual machines?

00:02:17.820 --> 00:02:19.530
Should they use BigQuery?

00:02:19.530 --> 00:02:22.570
Should they use
higher level services?

00:02:22.570 --> 00:02:26.030
How are they going to
understand DevOps practices?

00:02:26.030 --> 00:02:29.530
How are they going to work
with configuration as code?

00:02:29.530 --> 00:02:31.600
And really importantly,
how are they

00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:34.330
going to learn about
service cost control?

00:02:34.330 --> 00:02:37.668
You can make a very
expensive mistake very fast

00:02:37.668 --> 00:02:38.710
with this amount of data.

00:02:41.480 --> 00:02:44.770
Speaking of data,
how are we going

00:02:44.770 --> 00:02:47.290
to try out and learn
these services?

00:02:47.290 --> 00:02:50.680
The data that many of
my clients work with

00:02:50.680 --> 00:02:55.200
is personally identifiable
human health data.

00:02:55.200 --> 00:02:57.990
How are we going to work
with downsampled data sets?

00:02:57.990 --> 00:02:59.350
These data sets are huge.

00:02:59.350 --> 00:03:02.580
They have 3 billion letters.

00:03:02.580 --> 00:03:07.710
And they do analysis
across the genome.

00:03:07.710 --> 00:03:11.490
Speaking of the genome,
researchers really

00:03:11.490 --> 00:03:13.870
want to work with
genomics examples.

00:03:13.870 --> 00:03:16.350
Most of the examples in
Learning Cloud Services

00:03:16.350 --> 00:03:18.680
are generic, hello world.

00:03:18.680 --> 00:03:22.950
And that really doesn't
resonate my customers.

00:03:22.950 --> 00:03:26.130
On top of that,
in genomics, there

00:03:26.130 --> 00:03:32.160
is an increasing approach moving
towards tools and libraries

00:03:32.160 --> 00:03:35.050
such as GATK, the
Genomic Analysis Tool

00:03:35.050 --> 00:03:40.100
Kit, and the "widdle," or WDL,
Workflow Definition Language,

00:03:40.100 --> 00:03:43.400
the next Nextflow
library and language, NF,

00:03:43.400 --> 00:03:44.520
and Galaxy Project.

00:03:44.520 --> 00:03:45.620
There are many others.

00:03:48.510 --> 00:03:50.960
So what I've done to address
this knowledge gap is,

00:03:50.960 --> 00:03:54.170
I've created an open-source
course on GitHub.

00:03:54.170 --> 00:03:57.320
It is 100% free,
because it's on GitHub.

00:03:57.320 --> 00:04:02.420
And it consists of notes marked
on pages, screencasts, code

00:04:02.420 --> 00:04:04.760
examples, and sample data.

00:04:04.760 --> 00:04:08.920
Importantly, all of the
examples use genomic data.

00:04:08.920 --> 00:04:13.340
It's called GCP
for Bioinformatics.

00:04:13.340 --> 00:04:16.450
So this course
introduces researchers

00:04:16.450 --> 00:04:20.260
to working with raw Google
Cloud infrastructure

00:04:20.260 --> 00:04:24.220
or systems for genomic analysis
built on the Google Cloud,

00:04:24.220 --> 00:04:28.300
including Tera.bio from the
Broad Institute in [INAUDIBLE],,

00:04:28.300 --> 00:04:31.900
VarianceSpark Library from
CSIRO Bioinformatics in Sydney,

00:04:31.900 --> 00:04:35.890
Australia, Nextflow.Io
from the group in Europe,

00:04:35.890 --> 00:04:39.790
Galaxy Project, which
is global, and others.

00:04:39.790 --> 00:04:42.260
Now, to get an understanding
of the complexity,

00:04:42.260 --> 00:04:45.790
let's first consider batch
compute for analysis.

00:04:45.790 --> 00:04:47.620
If you start at the
bottom of the diagram,

00:04:47.620 --> 00:04:49.787
although today we're talking
about the Google Cloud,

00:04:49.787 --> 00:04:53.050
many of my researchers are faced
with the additional complexity

00:04:53.050 --> 00:04:55.180
of collaborating with
researchers who work

00:04:55.180 --> 00:04:57.080
with a different vendor cloud.

00:04:57.080 --> 00:04:59.620
So we have a number of
public vendor clouds.

00:04:59.620 --> 00:05:02.260
On top of that, they'll
build their analysis

00:05:02.260 --> 00:05:06.310
on clusters of virtual machines,
and increasingly, containers.

00:05:06.310 --> 00:05:08.140
And on top of that,
many of them are now

00:05:08.140 --> 00:05:10.390
starting to use orchestration
layers, such as Spark

00:05:10.390 --> 00:05:12.960
or Kubernetes.

00:05:12.960 --> 00:05:15.900
On top of all that
cloud infrastructure,

00:05:15.900 --> 00:05:19.470
researchers are often using
these bioinformatics tools

00:05:19.470 --> 00:05:21.250
and libraries that I
previously mentioned,

00:05:21.250 --> 00:05:24.480
such as Tera.bio,
Nextflow, Galaxy, and more.

00:05:24.480 --> 00:05:26.760
Those libraries have
associated languages,

00:05:26.760 --> 00:05:29.970
such as "widdle," WDL,
CWL, Common Workflow

00:05:29.970 --> 00:05:33.030
Language, Nextflow, NF, or GA.

00:05:33.030 --> 00:05:36.370
And those tools often
have orchestration layers,

00:05:36.370 --> 00:05:39.900
such as Cromwell for
Tera.bio or MiniWDL.

00:05:39.900 --> 00:05:42.690
Pipeline's API is
actually a Google API

00:05:42.690 --> 00:05:44.670
that works in
conjunction with a lot

00:05:44.670 --> 00:05:46.770
of these higher-level libraries.

00:05:46.770 --> 00:05:47.850
Does this look confusing?

00:05:47.850 --> 00:05:48.610
Well, it is.

00:05:48.610 --> 00:05:50.760
There is a lot of different
tools and libraries

00:05:50.760 --> 00:05:51.400
to understand.

00:05:51.400 --> 00:05:53.400
It's one of the reasons
that I made this course,

00:05:53.400 --> 00:05:56.400
to break all these services
into parts and pieces

00:05:56.400 --> 00:05:59.460
so that they can be learned
and understood individually

00:05:59.460 --> 00:06:01.840
and then combined effectively.

00:06:01.840 --> 00:06:04.890
Now, in addition
to batch compute,

00:06:04.890 --> 00:06:07.660
you'll notice a similar but
non-identical kind of pattern

00:06:07.660 --> 00:06:08.160
here.

00:06:08.160 --> 00:06:10.320
And this is
interactive analysis.

00:06:10.320 --> 00:06:13.200
Now, this keys around
the center square,

00:06:13.200 --> 00:06:16.150
in the center of the center
square, the iPython Notebook,

00:06:16.150 --> 00:06:16.650
or ipymb.

00:06:19.250 --> 00:06:21.650
It is becoming a
pattern in genomics

00:06:21.650 --> 00:06:25.040
to create reproducible research
in the form of a Jupyter

00:06:25.040 --> 00:06:26.120
Notebook.

00:06:26.120 --> 00:06:29.360
And so an important
pattern in pipelining

00:06:29.360 --> 00:06:31.700
is scaling those
notebook environments

00:06:31.700 --> 00:06:35.730
using Jupyter Hub
or Google Colabs.

00:06:35.730 --> 00:06:38.480
So again, there are a
number of parts and pieces

00:06:38.480 --> 00:06:41.150
to working with these
pipelining infrastructures.

00:06:41.150 --> 00:06:45.590
And my course covers all of
these on the Google Cloud.

00:06:45.590 --> 00:06:49.190
So drilling into the highlights
of what my course covers,

00:06:49.190 --> 00:06:51.817
it really is designed to
be consumed in the way

00:06:51.817 --> 00:06:53.400
that you actually
work with the cloud.

00:06:53.400 --> 00:06:55.970
There is a level zero for
setting up your account

00:06:55.970 --> 00:06:57.210
and setting up cost control.

00:06:57.210 --> 00:06:59.210
Level one is files and
data, because, of course,

00:06:59.210 --> 00:07:01.010
we're computing on files.

00:07:01.010 --> 00:07:04.910
And I cover core services,
such as storage buckets, Google

00:07:04.910 --> 00:07:06.110
Cloud Storage.

00:07:06.110 --> 00:07:08.340
I cover public data
sets that are available,

00:07:08.340 --> 00:07:10.300
such as 1,000 Genomes
and other data

00:07:10.300 --> 00:07:12.380
sets for genomics
that have restricted

00:07:12.380 --> 00:07:15.350
access, services
like BigQuery, which

00:07:15.350 --> 00:07:19.910
provides serverless SQL
querying on top of files

00:07:19.910 --> 00:07:24.650
and can be used for genomic type
files, such as VCF, or Variant

00:07:24.650 --> 00:07:26.360
Calling Format.

00:07:26.360 --> 00:07:29.120
Also, I cover higher level
services, such as Tera.bio

00:07:29.120 --> 00:07:30.260
reference data sets.

00:07:32.840 --> 00:07:35.780
In the compute
area, I cover topics

00:07:35.780 --> 00:07:38.345
such as virtual machines,
running containers,

00:07:38.345 --> 00:07:40.640
and of course, clusters
of each of these,

00:07:40.640 --> 00:07:44.360
and also include information
about serverless or functions

00:07:44.360 --> 00:07:47.570
at the workflow level,
but also the Google life

00:07:47.570 --> 00:07:51.680
sciences, API which is called
the Pipelines API, Nextflow,

00:07:51.680 --> 00:07:53.735
and higher level services
such as Tera.bio.

00:07:56.460 --> 00:08:01.260
Additionally, to cover
the interactive pattern,

00:08:01.260 --> 00:08:04.020
I have information about
working with and setting up

00:08:04.020 --> 00:08:07.850
Jupyter Notebook environments
on the Google Cloud platform.

00:08:07.850 --> 00:08:12.290
I also include modern DevOps
practices such as CI/CD,

00:08:12.290 --> 00:08:16.060
or Continuous Integration
and Continuous Deployment.

00:08:16.060 --> 00:08:18.670
And I have information
about machine learning

00:08:18.670 --> 00:08:22.240
that's specific to
genomics, covering examples

00:08:22.240 --> 00:08:27.790
of libraries like Deep Variant,
TensorFlow IO, and Google

00:08:27.790 --> 00:08:29.230
Nucleus.

00:08:29.230 --> 00:08:31.720
Another aspect of
the course design

00:08:31.720 --> 00:08:35.440
is that my examples are
designed to be quick.

00:08:35.440 --> 00:08:37.419
One of the challenges
that I found

00:08:37.419 --> 00:08:41.140
when I was learning genomics
tools as a cloud architect is

00:08:41.140 --> 00:08:43.720
many of the examples
reflected real world.

00:08:43.720 --> 00:08:47.450
And real world can be
huge amounts of data.

00:08:47.450 --> 00:08:51.010
So a hello world can take
minutes, or hours, or even

00:08:51.010 --> 00:08:52.590
days to run.

00:08:52.590 --> 00:08:56.170
And that just wasn't a
way that I wanted to work.

00:08:56.170 --> 00:08:59.360
So I provide example data.

00:08:59.360 --> 00:09:03.590
And I provide specific steps
to try out each service.

00:09:03.590 --> 00:09:06.950
My course is designed to run on
a trial Google Cloud Platform

00:09:06.950 --> 00:09:08.000
account.

00:09:08.000 --> 00:09:09.810
And if you run with
my example data sets,

00:09:09.810 --> 00:09:11.600
it should be a nearly no cost.

00:09:11.600 --> 00:09:14.000
I never pay more than $1 or $2.

00:09:14.000 --> 00:09:17.317
And importantly, most examples
run in five minutes or less.

00:09:17.317 --> 00:09:18.900
So you can quickly
get up and running.

00:09:18.900 --> 00:09:20.358
And you can find
out if the service

00:09:20.358 --> 00:09:23.600
is going to work for your
particular analysis needs.

00:09:23.600 --> 00:09:26.330
Another aspect to the way I've
designed the course is I've

00:09:26.330 --> 00:09:30.030
designed it at three levels
for each of the service areas,

00:09:30.030 --> 00:09:32.210
so for example, for files,
for data, for compute,

00:09:32.210 --> 00:09:33.930
so on and so forth.

00:09:33.930 --> 00:09:37.320
So the three levels are
infrastructure as a service.

00:09:37.320 --> 00:09:40.100
And this is for researchers
who want to set up, control,

00:09:40.100 --> 00:09:42.170
and manage everything.

00:09:42.170 --> 00:09:43.715
The level of data,
for example, they

00:09:43.715 --> 00:09:47.420
might want to set up their own
RDBMS, or relational database.

00:09:47.420 --> 00:09:51.240
They might want to set up
their own NoSQL environment

00:09:51.240 --> 00:09:55.440
on virtual machines or on
Google Cloud Storage buckets.

00:09:55.440 --> 00:09:57.870
At the compute layer,
these researchers

00:09:57.870 --> 00:10:01.440
would prefer to set up their
own clusters of Google Compute

00:10:01.440 --> 00:10:04.790
Engine virtual machines
or Docker images on Google

00:10:04.790 --> 00:10:07.570
Kubernetes Engine.

00:10:07.570 --> 00:10:09.970
There are other researchers
who would prefer

00:10:09.970 --> 00:10:12.260
to work at the platform level.

00:10:12.260 --> 00:10:15.910
They would rather run their
analysis over managed services

00:10:15.910 --> 00:10:20.570
and let Google handle more of
the underlying infrastructure.

00:10:20.570 --> 00:10:22.390
So data, this looks
like data lakes

00:10:22.390 --> 00:10:24.880
that are running on GCS
buckets, Google Cloud Storage

00:10:24.880 --> 00:10:29.140
buckets, and use of public
reference genomics data.

00:10:29.140 --> 00:10:31.750
At the compute layer,
this often manifests

00:10:31.750 --> 00:10:35.290
by using services such as
Dataproc or the Google Life

00:10:35.290 --> 00:10:40.840
Sciences API, which efficiently
handles orchestration of groups

00:10:40.840 --> 00:10:44.050
of virtual machines, allowing
the researchers to dynamically

00:10:44.050 --> 00:10:48.370
spin up and spin down
compute resources.

00:10:48.370 --> 00:10:49.870
And it allows them
to take advantage

00:10:49.870 --> 00:10:53.110
of aspects that are
specific to Google Cloud,

00:10:53.110 --> 00:10:55.600
such as preemptable
instances, so that they

00:10:55.600 --> 00:11:01.060
can save lots of money, which is
really important at this scale.

00:11:01.060 --> 00:11:03.760
Still, other researchers
prefer to work at the software

00:11:03.760 --> 00:11:05.200
as a service level.

00:11:05.200 --> 00:11:09.490
And one example of this is
using the Tera.bio system,

00:11:09.490 --> 00:11:11.230
which is a web UI
that was designed

00:11:11.230 --> 00:11:14.080
by the Broad institute in
[INAUDIBLE] that currently runs

00:11:14.080 --> 00:11:16.870
on top of the Google
Cloud Platform.

00:11:16.870 --> 00:11:20.650
In this case, rather than
interacting with the GCP

00:11:20.650 --> 00:11:25.180
console or using
tools such as gsutil,

00:11:25.180 --> 00:11:28.480
my researchers will go
to the Tera.bio platform.

00:11:28.480 --> 00:11:31.150
And they can access
both their own data

00:11:31.150 --> 00:11:35.550
and publicly available genomic
data through the Tera UI.

00:11:35.550 --> 00:11:40.260
Also they can spin up and spin
down Jupyter Notebook instances

00:11:40.260 --> 00:11:45.210
and compute clusters that run
on top of the Life Sciences API

00:11:45.210 --> 00:11:47.470
through the web UI.

00:11:47.470 --> 00:11:50.510
Now, that's a lot of different
areas to think about.

00:11:50.510 --> 00:11:52.870
So I think at this
point, it will be helpful

00:11:52.870 --> 00:11:59.330
if we actually take just a brief
look at the course on GitHub.

00:11:59.330 --> 00:12:03.560
In this demo, I want to show
you my open-source course that I

00:12:03.560 --> 00:12:06.090
built, GCP for Bioinformatics.

00:12:06.090 --> 00:12:09.620
So first, we're going to go
out to the site on GitHub.

00:12:09.620 --> 00:12:12.080
And this is where all the
artifacts for the course

00:12:12.080 --> 00:12:13.620
are located.

00:12:13.620 --> 00:12:17.030
You can see that the course
is organized by topic area.

00:12:17.030 --> 00:12:18.770
And it's set up in folders.

00:12:18.770 --> 00:12:21.770
So they're numbered
in a logical order.

00:12:21.770 --> 00:12:25.050
You would, of course, start by
setting up your GCP account.

00:12:25.050 --> 00:12:26.818
Then you would work
with files of data.

00:12:26.818 --> 00:12:29.360
Then you would work with virtual
machines, Docker containers,

00:12:29.360 --> 00:12:33.090
or functions, so compute,
and multiple instances.

00:12:33.090 --> 00:12:35.130
You may add machine learning.

00:12:35.130 --> 00:12:37.820
You also might use
code and cloud service

00:12:37.820 --> 00:12:40.070
tools, such as
continuous integration

00:12:40.070 --> 00:12:43.370
and continuous deployment,
container registries,

00:12:43.370 --> 00:12:45.280
and other tools.

00:12:45.280 --> 00:12:47.740
In each section,
the organization

00:12:47.740 --> 00:12:50.080
is set up as follows.

00:12:50.080 --> 00:12:52.300
At the beginning of each
section, there is a read me.

00:12:52.300 --> 00:12:56.010
So there is a main read me.

00:12:56.010 --> 00:12:58.090
I'll just scroll down here.

00:12:58.090 --> 00:13:00.000
And you can see that
I've consistently

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:04.050
used Emoji throughout
the repository

00:13:04.050 --> 00:13:06.150
to make it easier to scan.

00:13:06.150 --> 00:13:08.160
You can see the list here.

00:13:08.160 --> 00:13:10.320
As it says, this repo
includes content you

00:13:10.320 --> 00:13:13.050
can read, watch, or run.

00:13:13.050 --> 00:13:15.150
So you can read
pages of the repo.

00:13:15.150 --> 00:13:16.650
You can think of them as notes.

00:13:16.650 --> 00:13:19.200
You can watch linked
YouTube screencasts.

00:13:19.200 --> 00:13:20.940
They're demonstrations.

00:13:20.940 --> 00:13:23.640
You can try out the
computational examples

00:13:23.640 --> 00:13:25.890
using Jupyter Notebooks.

00:13:25.890 --> 00:13:28.740
There are linked GitHub repos.

00:13:28.740 --> 00:13:32.130
And there are additional
advanced topic links

00:13:32.130 --> 00:13:35.080
at the end of each section.

00:13:35.080 --> 00:13:39.910
On each page, there will
be a linked YouTube video.

00:13:39.910 --> 00:13:43.680
So you just click the
graphic to go to the video.

00:13:43.680 --> 00:13:46.800
If you prefer to learn
by watching screencasts,

00:13:46.800 --> 00:13:48.870
you can simply go
to the playlists

00:13:48.870 --> 00:13:50.790
that I've created and linked.

00:13:50.790 --> 00:13:53.970
And as you can see, there are
27 different short screencasts,

00:13:53.970 --> 00:13:56.880
mostly around five minutes--
some of them are six or seven--

00:13:56.880 --> 00:14:00.020
that cover the topics
via demonstrations.

00:14:00.020 --> 00:14:03.730
Now, just to get an idea of
how each topic is set up,

00:14:03.730 --> 00:14:05.902
we'll take a look
at one topic area

00:14:05.902 --> 00:14:07.860
just so you can understand
the structure, which

00:14:07.860 --> 00:14:11.470
is consistent
throughout the course.

00:14:11.470 --> 00:14:15.010
So here we have in the
files and data section--

00:14:15.010 --> 00:14:19.590
in that section, we have a
topic called SQL questions.

00:14:19.590 --> 00:14:24.540
And this topic is covering
using the BigQuery service

00:14:24.540 --> 00:14:27.720
with a bioinformatics
or genomic example.

00:14:27.720 --> 00:14:30.930
So you can see, first, there is
a little explanation about it.

00:14:30.930 --> 00:14:34.290
And then these
notes pages are very

00:14:34.290 --> 00:14:38.040
action-oriented in the
design, in that they're

00:14:38.040 --> 00:14:41.310
created so that you
can actually try out

00:14:41.310 --> 00:14:45.360
the service with a genomics
example within 5 to 10 minutes,

00:14:45.360 --> 00:14:46.987
and get a result
that quickly as well.

00:14:46.987 --> 00:14:48.320
So this is a little bit unusual.

00:14:48.320 --> 00:14:50.320
And that's one of the
reasons I made the course,

00:14:50.320 --> 00:14:53.400
because I've seen a lot
of bioinformatics examples

00:14:53.400 --> 00:14:55.350
that take hours to run.

00:14:55.350 --> 00:14:58.140
And I really, in
course design, wanted

00:14:58.140 --> 00:15:01.200
to create examples that
would cost basically nothing

00:15:01.200 --> 00:15:04.650
to try out because of the tiny
sizes of the sample data sets

00:15:04.650 --> 00:15:07.360
and would be just
really quick and easy.

00:15:07.360 --> 00:15:08.940
Now, in the case
of BigQuery, I've

00:15:08.940 --> 00:15:12.480
included a number of
screenshots in this notes page,

00:15:12.480 --> 00:15:15.640
because I'm assuming that, for
most people taking the course,

00:15:15.640 --> 00:15:18.750
this will be a new service.

00:15:18.750 --> 00:15:22.160
So you can see, for example,
I've used this screenshot

00:15:22.160 --> 00:15:25.640
in the beginning of this page.

00:15:25.640 --> 00:15:28.960
Now, this page
actually is a link out

00:15:28.960 --> 00:15:30.790
to an additional course.

00:15:30.790 --> 00:15:33.460
And this is a course
on using the ANSI SQL

00:15:33.460 --> 00:15:37.390
language against a
sample genomic data set.

00:15:37.390 --> 00:15:39.247
And if you just
go down the page,

00:15:39.247 --> 00:15:41.830
this page is a little bit longer
than some of the other pages,

00:15:41.830 --> 00:15:44.560
because it's a
three-part course.

00:15:44.560 --> 00:15:47.680
And you can see, for example,
we've got SQL queries here

00:15:47.680 --> 00:15:49.390
that you can actually run.

00:15:49.390 --> 00:15:52.780
Now, just to show you how
this is intended to be used,

00:15:52.780 --> 00:15:58.060
the course is designed to
be used with a trial GCP

00:15:58.060 --> 00:16:02.210
actual account so you can run
the lessons and learn by doing.

00:16:02.210 --> 00:16:03.940
So for this particular
one, if I scroll

00:16:03.940 --> 00:16:06.330
way down here for a second--

00:16:06.330 --> 00:16:07.330
and I'll just stop here.

00:16:07.330 --> 00:16:10.840
This is an example using a
concept called a self-join

00:16:10.840 --> 00:16:17.150
on a very, very small, but
a domain-specific example.

00:16:17.150 --> 00:16:19.840
And so the idea here is
to return a hierarchy.

00:16:19.840 --> 00:16:23.290
And the lesson,
as you can see, is

00:16:23.290 --> 00:16:25.495
write a SQL query to return
the grandparent category

00:16:25.495 --> 00:16:27.830
of glycine binding.

00:16:27.830 --> 00:16:30.640
So to show you the environment
where you'd work with this,

00:16:30.640 --> 00:16:32.980
this is BigQuery in GCP.

00:16:32.980 --> 00:16:37.900
And for this course, I've
created a publicly available

00:16:37.900 --> 00:16:39.710
really small sample data set.

00:16:39.710 --> 00:16:42.970
So you can see here, I'll click
on this experiments table.

00:16:42.970 --> 00:16:44.520
And I can actually preview it.

00:16:44.520 --> 00:16:47.470
And it has just
five rows, again,

00:16:47.470 --> 00:16:49.670
really tiny examples here.

00:16:49.670 --> 00:16:51.940
One of the concepts,
as I've mentioned,

00:16:51.940 --> 00:16:54.310
in talking about this
course is that you

00:16:54.310 --> 00:16:57.910
can explore these services
with examples that come

00:16:57.910 --> 00:17:00.160
from genomics that are tiny.

00:17:00.160 --> 00:17:02.590
So they can do it
basically at no cost

00:17:02.590 --> 00:17:05.540
and in just a short
amount of time.

00:17:05.540 --> 00:17:08.560
So the course is
completely on GitHub.

00:17:08.560 --> 00:17:10.430
So it's completely free.

00:17:10.430 --> 00:17:12.190
It's completely open source.

00:17:12.190 --> 00:17:15.240
I really welcome any
suggestions from the community.

00:17:15.240 --> 00:17:17.814
The best way to do that is
